      "math_academy_id": "q-129290",
      "sequence_position": 18,
      "problem": "Find ${\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+\\,j+2\\pi \\,k$ \nand \n$c=2\\,i-\\,k.$",
      "worked_solution": "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2\\,i-\\,k)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+\\,j+2\\pi \\,k) \\\\  & =\\pi -2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
